                              <abbr>ይተ፡, ይተረ፡,  ይተረጐ፡</abbr> for <expan>ይተረጐም፡ </expan> (e.g., <locus target="#129 #130 #131"/>)</item>
                        </list>
                        <seg type="rubrication">Divine names; two initial lines on the incipit page of Texts I, II, III, IV; two initial lines of some sections of Text I;
                           two pairs of initial lines of section I-3 alternating with black lines;
                           two initial lines of section I-2; elements of punctuation signs;
                           elements of Ethiopic numerals; quire marks.Some terms and words,
                           like <foreign xml:lang="gez">ḥatatā</foreign> (e.g., <locus target="#1rv #3rv #4rv"/>), <foreign xml:lang="gez">taśaṭwo</foreign> (e.g., <locus from="1" to="4"/>),
                           <foreign xml:lang="gez">ʾǝm-maṣḥaf</foreign> (<locus target="#13v"/>), <foreign xml:lang="gez">tǝrgʷāme</foreign> (<locus target="#13v"/>), 
                           <foreign xml:lang="gez">zǝntu wǝʾǝtu</foreign> (<locus target="#21r"/>), <foreign xml:lang="gez">kǝfǝl qadāmāwi</foreign> (<locus target="#11r"/>), 
                           <foreign xml:lang="gez">tafṣāmita mǝnbāb</foreign> (<locus target="#31r"/>).</seg>
                     </handNote>
                  </handDesc>
                  <additions>
                     <list>
                        
                        <item xml:id="a1">
                           <locus target="#132v"/>
                           <desc>Note on types and qualities of precious stones. The note is written in the same hand as Additio 2. </desc>
                           <quote xml:lang="gez">እጽሕፍ፡ ነገረ፡ አዕናቍ፡ ቀይሐን፡ ወኅብረ፡ ደም፡ ወጸዓዳን፡ ወሐመልሚላን፡ ወኅብረ፡ ስሞሙሂ፡ ጳዝዮን፡<gap reason="ellipsis" resp="AA"/> </quote>
                           <quote xml:lang="en">I write the account of precious stones: red ones, those like blood, white ones, green ones, and there
                              common name is Ṗazǝyon</quote>
                          
                        </item>
                        <item xml:id="a2">
                           <locus target="#132v"/>
                           <desc>Note on symbolic interpretation of Biblical peoples as different animals.</desc>
                           <quote xml:lang="gez">
                              <locus target="#132v"/>ምሳሌ፡ አሕዛብ፡ አናብስት፡ ባቢሎን፡ አናምርት፡ ሚዶን፡ ድብ፡ ፋርስ፡ አዝዕብት፡ ግብጽ፡ አክልብት፡ ኢሎፍሊ፡ <gap reason="ellipsis" resp="AA"></gap></quote>
                           <quote xml:lang="en">Symbol of peoples: Babylon <supplied reason="subaudible">is like</supplied> Lion, Midon <supplied reason="subaudible">is like</supplied> tigers, Persia
                              <supplied reason="subaudible">is like</supplied> a bear, Egypt <supplied reason="subaudible">is like</supplied> hyenas, Philistines (ʾIlofli) <supplied reason="subaudible">are like</supplied> dogs.</quote>
                           <note> The note is written in the same hand as Additio 1. </note>
                        </item>
                        <item xml:id="e1">- The title of Text I is crudely written on the front
                           board with yellow paint.&#xD; - Corrections written interlineally: <locus target="#7v">7v</locus>, 8r, 28v, 29r, 53r.&#xD; - Immediate
                           corrections marked with dashes and thin lines above and below the word:
                              <locus target="#17v">17v</locus>, 21v, 22r, 43r, 50r, 53r.&#xD; -
                           Erased words: <locus target="#78v">78v</locus>, 82r, 124v.</item>
                        <item xml:id="e2">shelfmark C4-IV-60 (<locus target="#89r">89r</locus>)</item>
                     </list>
